tal"}],"notes":"David Chipperfield's Turner Contemporary (2011) on Margate's seafront, from published descriptions and the ground file (a strip about 80 x 40 m along the sea wall, turned about 38 degrees): six identical two-storey volumes clad in white opal glass, in two rows of three on a raised concrete plinth, each under a mono-pitched roof rising toward the sea, so the end elevation is a double sawtooth; a large picture window to the sea, the glazed entrance on the town side. Refined 29 Sep 2026 (no photographs reachable; Wikimedia still blocked): the joints between the volumes widened to 1.8 m and glazed low down, so the six read as six; the north lights put in, the back row's high glass face looking to sea over the front row's low roof; steps from the plinth down to the sands at the south-west end; and the setting to the north-east from the ground file and published maps: the tidal harbour basin, the Harbour Arm running on along the line of the sea wall, and the white Droit House at its root. Still guessed: the volumes' exact dimensions and pitches, window positions, the Droit House's form, and where the arm begins."}